# Part A of guide rows. Each A() call has exactly 12 arguments.
def load(A):
    A("no-hot-water","No hot water in a coast house","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "What to check when a Lincoln or Tillamook County house has no hot water, and when to call.",
      "Start with the pattern",
      "No hot water at every faucet points at the heater, its breaker, or its fuel. Hot water in the shower and none at one sink points at that sink. Write the pattern down before you call so the visit starts in the right place.",
      "Empty weeks add ordinary causes. A breaker may be off, a gas control may sit on pilot, or a tank may still be recovering after back-to-back showers. A puddle under the tank, a dripping relief valve, or heavy rust at the fittings means leave the unit off and call.",
      "Fuel and safety",
      "Electric tanks use elements and thermostats. Gas tanks use a burner, a vent, and a control. Do not open a burner chamber, and do not touch a panel that is wet. If you smell gas, leave the house and call the gas utility from outside.",
      "Plumbing 101, LLC lists water heater installation on its own site and on its BBB profile. Share the symptom, the fuel if you know it, and the town. Posted hours are Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.",
      ["Note which faucets are cold.","Look for a puddle without touching wiring.","Leave a wet heater off.","Have the town and a callback number ready."])
    A("water-heater-noises","Pops and rumbles from a water heater","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "What popping, rumbling, and hissing from a water heater usually mean.",
      "Sediment and kettling",
      "Popping or rumbling often means sediment on the bottom of the tank. Heat turns trapped water to steam and the tank knocks. A flush can help a tank that is otherwise sound. A flush does not fix a tank that is already leaking.",
      "A hiss near the top can be a relief valve or a fitting. Water from the relief pipe means the valve opened. That can be high pressure, high temperature, or a worn valve. Do not cap the relief pipe.",
      "Stop-using signs",
      "Scorch marks, a soot trail on a gas heater, or a melted electrical cover are reasons to leave the unit off. Water at the base that returns after you dry it is a call, not a towel problem. Surface rust on a fitting in damp coastal air is a watch item. Water on the floor is not.",
      "Say whether the heater is gas or electric, how old it is if the label shows a date, and whether a pan is holding water. The company installs water heaters and does not publish a flat price for that work.",
      ["Name the kind of sound.","Look at the pan and the relief pipe.","Do not cap a relief line.","Leave a soot-stained gas heater off."])
    A("anode-rods","Anode rods and tank life","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "A plain explanation of anode rods and why tank life is not the same in every house.",
      "The rod is meant to corrode",
      "Most glass-lined tanks have a rod that corrodes in place of the steel. When the rod is gone, the tank is next. Some water uses a rod up faster. A plumber can sometimes change a rod if the tank is still sound and there is room above the heater.",
      "A rod change does not fix a tank that already leaks through the shell. A rotten-egg smell on the hot side only, with normal cold water, is sometimes tied to the rod and the water. It is worth describing, and it is not a reason to ignore a drip.",
      "Room above the heater",
      "The rod can be nearly as long as the tank. Closets under stairs and crawlspace heaters often do not have the clearance to pull one. A garage with open space above the heater is easier. Say where the heater sits when you ask for an estimate.",
      "Vacation houses that sit idle still age. If the house is on a well, say so. Water heater installation is a listed service when the tank itself is finished.",
      ["Note clearance above the heater.","Say gas or electric.","Separate a hot-only smell from a house-wide smell.","Do not drain a leaking tank onto a finished floor."])
    A("relief-valve-drip","A dripping relief valve","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "Why a temperature and pressure relief valve drips, and what you can safely observe.",
      "Hot water needs room to expand",
      "Water expands as it heats. If a check valve, a pressure regulator, or the meter stops that water from moving back toward the street, pressure in the house rises. The relief valve is the safety outlet. A drip can mean high pressure, high temperature, or a tired valve.",
      "An expansion tank on the cold inlet gives the heated water somewhere to go. If that small tank is waterlogged, it stops helping. Do not cap the relief pipe, and do not add a shutoff to it.",
      "What a gauge can tell you",
      "A hose-bib gauge, if you already own one, shows static pressure. Many houses sit comfortably somewhere near 40 to 80 psi. Pressure that stays well above that range is hard on fill valves, supply lines, and heaters. Do not crank a regulator you do not know.",
      "Tell the plumber whether the drip follows a long shower or runs all day, and whether an expansion tank is already there. Pipe evaluation and water heater work are both on the published list.",
      ["Watch when the relief pipe drips.","Never cap the outlet.","Note a regulator on the main.","Say whether the supply is a well or a utility."])
    A("heater-pans","Water heater pans","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "What a pan under a water heater does, and why a full pan is not a finished repair.",
      "A pan catches water. It does not stop the leak.",
      "A pan under a tank keeps a slow leak off a finished floor. Some pans have a drain to the outside or to a floor drain. A full pan means the tank or a fitting is leaking, or the pan drain is plugged. Bailing the pan without finding the source only resets the clock.",
      "Garage and closet heaters on the coast sit in damp rooms. A rusty pan can look worse than the tank, and a clean pan can hide a leak at a union above it. Look at the fittings and the shell.",
      "If the heater was under water",
      "Gas heaters need air and a sound vent. Electric heaters need a dry connection. A heater that sat in floodwater in a crawlspace or garage should stay off. Otis, Nehalem, and other river spots have seen high water. Say if floodwater reached the unit.",
      "Plumbing 101, LLC installs water heaters. Describe the room, the fuel, and whether water is in the pan or past it.",
      ["See if the pan has a drain.","Dry the floor and watch what returns.","Leave a submerged heater off.","Photograph the fittings if you can do it safely."])
    A("heater-while-away","Water heaters in houses that sit empty","Water heaters","water-heaters",
      "How vacancy changes a water heater in a second home on these two counties.",
      "Idle weeks",
      "A heater left on in an empty house still stands by, so a small leak can run with nobody home. Some owners shut the water and the heater off between stays. Others leave both on. Tell the plumber which habit you use, because it explains a cold tank, a dry trap, or a surprise drip.",
      "Guest turnover hits fixtures in short peaks: several showers, then silence. Lukewarm water on a full morning can be recovery, sediment, or a tired thermostat. Rusty water or a wet tank is a different report. Say which one you see.",
      "Access for the visit",
      "If you are not there, leave a door code, a local contact, and the heater location. A closet blocked with storage delays the work. Posted hours are weekdays, 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. Plan the visit inside that window.",
      "The company serves Lincoln and Tillamook counties from Lincoln City. Installation and a smaller service call are different estimates. Ask which one you are requesting.",
      ["Say whether the house was vacant.","Say whether water and power stayed on.","Clear the path to the heater.","Leave a person who can meet the plumber."])
    A("slow-tub","A slow tub drain","Drains","drain-services",
      "How to tell a single slow tub from a wider drain problem.",
      "One fixture or several",
      "A tub that drains slowly while the sink and toilet in that room behave is often hair, a sticky stopper, or a short run of pipe. If the tub, the sink, and a floor drain rise together, the blockage is farther downstream.",
      "A toilet that gurgles when the tub empties is a clue. So is a floor drain that bubbles. Write down the order of events. That sequence helps a plumber choose between a trap and a main line.",
      "Cleaners and ceilings",
      "Chemical openers can sit in a trap and burn skin later. Hair on a stopper is a better clue than a second bottle. Do not take a trap apart if the fitting is seized. A new stain on the ceiling below is a reason to stop and call.",
      "Drain and sink service is on the BBB list. Say which fixtures, which floor, and whether this started after guests, a storm, or a remodel.",
      ["List every slow fixture.","Note gurgling or a rising floor drain.","Skip a second dose of chemical opener.","Mention a ceiling stain."])
    A("kitchen-backup","A kitchen sink that will not empty","Drains","drain-services",
      "Ordinary reasons a kitchen sink backs up, and what to say when you call.",
      "Grease, grounds, and the hose",
      "Kitchen lines collect grease and food bits. Grease cools on the pipe wall. A double bowl that is slow on both sides usually shares a line past the trap. One bowl slow and one bowl fine can be a stopper, or a dishwasher hose pushed too far into the tailpiece.",
      "A disposal that hums and does not spin should be switched off. Many units have a hex socket on the bottom that takes an Allen key with the power off. If you are unsure, leave it alone.",
      "The dishwasher connection",
      "A dishwasher that puddles the floor may be dumping into a clogged sink line. The drain hose should rise in a high loop or to an air gap before it drops. A hose lying flat can let sink water run back into the machine.",
      "Kitchen plumbing is listed. Mention a disposal, a dishwasher, and whether the house is on a sewer or a septic tank. Septic pumping is not a listed service. The drain inside the house is.",
      ["See if one bowl or both are slow.","Check the hose path only if the cabinet is dry.","Stop running water into a full sink.","Say sewer or septic."])
    A("dry-trap-smell","Sewer smell after a vacancy","Drains","drain-services",
      "Why an empty beach house smells like a drain, and the trap check that often clears it.",
      "The water plug dried out",
      "Every fixture trap holds water so sewer gas stays in the pipe. Fans and empty weeks dry floor drains, showers, and unused tubs. The smell hits when you open the door. A pitcher of water in that drain often restores the plug.",
      "If the smell stays after every trap is refilled, you may have a trap you have not found, a missing cleanout cap, a vent issue, or a real sewer leak. A smell with a wet ceiling is urgent.",
      "Garage and utility drains",
      "Coast garages often have a floor drain nobody uses. It is a prime suspect after a vacancy. A trap primer is supposed to add water on its own, and primers fail quietly. Refilling by hand is the homeowner test.",
      "Call if water in the traps does not clear the smell, or if several drains are slow together. Drain service is on the published list.",
      ["Pour water in unused showers and floor drains.","Replace a missing cleanout cap only if you have the right plug.","Note whether the smell is one room or the whole house.","Call if a ceiling is wet."])
    A("main-line-signs","When the problem is past one fixture","Drains","drain-services",
      "Symptoms that point past a stopper to a branch or main drain.",
      "The pattern",
      "Several fixtures on the lowest floor backing up together is the usual main-line picture. An upstairs sink that drains while a downstairs shower rises is another. A toilet that struggles only after the washer discharges ties those two fixtures to the same downstream pipe.",
      "Roots, a settled line, or a heavy grease load show up in older towns. You do not have to name the cause. Report the pattern, any recent digging, and whether a cleanout has overflowed.",
      "Cleanout caps",
      "A cleanout is a capped fitting on the drain, often in a garage, a wall, or just outside. If it leaks when fixtures run, that is useful information. Do not remove a cap while lines are full. Sewage under pressure will use that opening.",
      "Pipe inspection and drain service are both listed. An inspection is how a repeating backup gets looked at instead of guessed.",
      ["Note which floor backs up.","Watch one washer cycle.","Do not open a cleanout that is pushing.","Mention trees near the sewer line."])
    A("drain-donts","What does not belong in the drain","Drains","drain-services",
      "Items that turn into drain calls in coast houses and rentals.",
      "The usual list",
      "Wipes labeled flushable still catch in older lines. Grease poured in a sink cools and narrows the pipe. Coffee grounds, eggshells, and pasta settle or swell. Cat litter belongs in the trash. Sand from beach showers packs into traps.",
      "An outdoor shower deserves a screen you can lift and shake. A hair catcher in an indoor shower is the same idea. Both are cheaper than a cable.",
      "Guests",
      "Turnover raises the odds. A short house note about wipes and grease prevents some calls. It does not clear a line that is already full. If a backup starts, stop running water at that fixture.",
      "Drain and sink service is listed work. If you know sand, grease, or a toy went down, say so.",
      ["Put grease in a can.","Bag wipes and litter.","Shake sand out of shower screens.","Stop using a fixture that is rising."])
    A("washer-standpipe","Washer overflows and standpipes","Drains","drain-services",
      "How to tell a washer supply leak from a drain overflow.",
      "Two different spills",
      "Supply water keeps coming until a valve closes. Drain water arrives in a rush during pump-out and then stops. A standpipe that is too short, a lint clog, or a hose shoved in like a cork will dump that pump-out on the floor.",
      "Washer hoses age. A bulge is a reason to replace the hose if the valves close and hold. If the valves will not close, that is a plumbing call, not a hardware-store errand.",
      "The pipe itself",
      "The hose should not seal the standpipe airtight, and the pipe should be tall enough that the pump does not fountain out. Laundry in a garage collects extra dust in that line. Mention whether the spill was hot and constant or soapy and timed with the drain cycle.",
      "Faucet repair and drain service are different tickets. The description tells them apart.",
      ["Shut washer valves if water is spraying.","Note whether the spill matches a drain cycle.","Look for a bulged hose.","Do not run another cycle into a full standpipe."])
    A("running-toilet","A toilet that will not stop running","Fixtures","toilet-repair",
      "How to tell a flapper leak from a fill valve, with a simple dye test.",
      "Listen and dye",
      "A running toilet usually means water slipping from the tank to the bowl, with the fill valve replacing it. A hiss that never stops after the tank looks full is often the fill valve. A refill every few minutes is often the flapper.",
      "Food coloring in the tank, with no flush, will tint the bowl if water is leaking past the flapper. That test does not require taking the toilet apart.",
      "When it is more than parts",
      "Coast damp ages rubber. A warped flapper is due. A toilet that rocks, a sewer smell at the base, or a soft floor means the wax seal or the flange may be involved. That is no longer a tank-parts visit. A wet supply line is urgent because it is under pressure.",
      "Toilet service and repair is on the BBB profile. Say whether the toilet runs, rocks, or leaks at the supply.",
      ["Dye the tank and wait without flushing.","See if water spills into the overflow tube.","Feel for rocking at the base.","Shut the angle stop if the supply line is wet."])
    A("rocking-toilet","A toilet that rocks","Fixtures","toilet-repair",
      "What movement at the base means for the wax ring, the flange, and the floor.",
      "Movement breaks the seal",
      "A toilet should sit still. Rocking means loose bolts, a low or broken flange, or a soft floor. Each flush can push a little water past a failed wax ring. You may not see it until the ceiling below stains or the flooring swells.",
      "A gentle snug on the bolts is reasonable if the floor is solid and the rock just started. Cranking a toilet on a spongy floor drives the flange through the damage. If the floor feels soft, stop.",
      "New flooring",
      "A flange that sits below the finished floor often needs an extender so the seal can reach. This shows up right after new flooring. If the toilet was reset the same day the floor went in, say so.",
      "Bathroom plumbing, including toilet repair, is listed. Mention the room below and any stain.",
      ["Do not overtighten bolts on a soft floor.","Look at the ceiling underneath.","Say if new flooring was just installed.","Avoid that toilet if water is at the base."])
    A("faucet-drip","A faucet that drips","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "How to describe a drip so the repair starts at the right part.",
      "Spout, handle, or cabinet",
      "A drip from the spout with the handle off is usually a cartridge, a stem, or a washer. A drip under the handle is a seal on that side. Water under the sink is a supply line, a spray hose, or the shank where the faucet meets the deck. Those are different parts.",
      "You do not have to name the cartridge. A photo from above and a photo of the piping underneath is enough to start an email estimate.",
      "Stops under the sink",
      "Most sinks have angle stops on hot and cold. If they turn and the drip stops, you can wait for a weekday visit without wasting water. If a stop spins and the water continues, leave it and use the house main if the leak is serious. Mention a failed stop so it can be replaced in the same visit.",
      "Faucet service is on the published list, along with kitchen and bathroom plumbing.",
      ["See where the water appears.","Photo the faucet and the pipes below.","Try the angle stops if they move easily.","Say if a stop failed to shut."])
    A("clogged-aerator","Low flow at one faucet","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "When weak flow is a clogged aerator, and when it is a whole-house problem.",
      "Compare fixtures",
      "If one faucet is weak and the shower is strong, unscrew the aerator on the spout. Sand, rust, and debris collect there after a shutoff or street work. Rinse the screen and put it back. If every cold faucet is weak, the aerator is not the cause.",
      "Whole-house weakness can be a partly closed main, a regulator, a meter issue, or a leak. Hot-only weakness can be a hot stop or something inside the heater. Say which pattern you have.",
      "After pipe work",
      "New pipe and new heaters shed debris for a short time. Aerators and shower heads catch it. Cleaning them once is normal. Cleaning them every day means debris is still moving, and the source needs a look.",
      "Faucet repair and pipe work are both listed. One faucet versus the whole house is the detail that sorts the visit.",
      ["Compare the weak faucet with a shower.","Clean one aerator and retest.","Note hot only, cold only, or both.","Call if the whole house lost pressure."])
    A("shower-swings","Shower temperature that swings","Fixtures","faucet-repair",
      "Why a shower goes hot when a toilet flushes, and what a balance valve is for.",
      "Pressure drops",
      "Older two-handle showers have no protection when a toilet fills or a washer starts. Cold pressure drops and the shower goes hot. A pressure-balance cartridge in a single-handle valve is supposed to hold the mix. When it sticks, the shower stays warm, blasts hot, or will not shut fully.",
      "A scald risk is a reason to stop using that shower, especially for children or older adults. Lowering the heater a few degrees can cut the maximum while you wait. It does not repair the valve.",
      "Do not pry a stuck cartridge",
      "Many cartridges come out through the handle trim. Scale on coastal and well water sticks them in place. Prying with the wrong tool cracks the valve body in the wall and turns a parts visit into an open-wall repair.",
      "Faucet and bathroom repairs are listed. Read the brand on the handle if it is printed there, and say whether the issue is scald, a drip, or no hot.",
      ["Stop using a shower that scalds.","Note the brand on the handle.","Say if a toilet flush changes the temperature.","Do not pry a stuck cartridge."])
    A("what-a-repipe-is","What a whole-home re-pipe includes","Pipes","repiping",
      "A plain description of a re-pipe, matching the work named publicly for this company.",
      "Supply pipes",
      "A re-pipe usually replaces the supply lines from the main shutoff to the fixtures. It is not automatically a new sewer. The company website carries a client note about a beach home that was re-piped. The BBB profile lists new piping and re-piping. The scope of one house is whatever the estimate says.",
      "Older coast houses may have galvanized pipe that has narrowed inside, or a mix of copper, steel, and later repairs. Low pressure, rusty water, and pinholes that move from fitting to fitting are the usual reasons people ask.",
      "Paths and patching",
      "Crawlspace houses often get new pipe below the floor, with short rises to fixtures. Slab houses may need overhead runs in closets. Closing drywall is a different task from pulling pipe. Ask who closes the walls.",
      "Call on weekdays between 8:30 a.m. and 3:30 p.m. Bring a list of fixtures and any known leak locations.",
      ["List rusty water, low pressure, or repeated leaks.","Note crawlspace, slab, or both.","Ask who patches openings.","Separate supply trouble from drain backups."])
    A("galvanized-pipe","Galvanized pipe in older houses","Pipes","repiping",
      "How galvanized supply pipe fails, and what you can see without opening a wall.",
      "It closes from the inside",
      "Galvanized steel rusts inward. Flow drops at the fixtures farthest from the main, and hot lines often worsen first. The outside can look dull but solid while the inside is nearly closed. A magnet sticks to galvanized steel and not to copper. That is a fair test on an exposed run.",
      "Weeping threads are a late stage. Replacing one nipple can be the right small repair. It is a weak plan if every exposed fitting is wet. Pipe inspection is listed so the choice can follow what is visible.",
      "Color of the water",
      "Brown water only on the hot side points at the heater or the hot steel lines. Brown water on hot and cold points at the shared supply. Run a bucket clear and see if the color returns the same day. Do not scrape old pipe insulation apart. Point it out and leave it intact.",
      "Re-piping is the listed service when the system, not one nipple, is the problem.",
      ["Test an exposed pipe with a magnet.","Note hot-only or whole-house rust.","Count how often pinholes appear.","Leave suspect insulation alone."])
    A("pex-and-copper","PEX and copper near salt air","Pipes","repiping",
      "A homeowner comparison of two common supply materials, without a one-size rule.",
      "What each one asks of the house",
      "Copper is rigid and familiar, and it can pinhole in some waters. PEX is flexible and uses fewer joints in many re-pipes. It needs the right fittings, support, and protection from sunlight. Both are normal. The choice is a job decision, not a slogan.",
      "Salt air attacks what is outdoors and what stays damp in an open crawlspace: steel hangers, valves, and exposed metal. That does not mean every house needs plastic. It does mean hose bibs and unpainted steel age faster here than in a dry inland closet.",
      "Questions for the estimate",
      "Ask which material is proposed, where shutoffs will sit, and how pipe is protected through studs. Keep the manufacturer sheet. Oregon work often needs a permit. Ask how permits are handled for your town instead of guessing a fee from a webpage.",
      "New piping and re-piping are listed services. The written estimate should name the scope.",
      ["Ask which material and why.","Ask where new shutoffs will sit.","Keep the product sheet.","Ask how the permit is handled."])
    A("find-shutoff","Find the main water shutoff","Pipes","residential-plumbing",
      "Where coast houses hide the main valve, and how to use it during a leak.",
      "Where to look",
      "Start where the line enters: a crawlspace wall, a garage, a utility closet, or a meter box at the property line. Houses on piers sometimes have the valve under the floor near the stairs. Well houses have a valve at the pressure tank. The house valve is the one on the building side of the meter.",
      "Ball valves turn a quarter turn. Gate valves take several turns and can fail if they have sat open for years. If a handle breaks during a flood, use the meter shutoff or call the water utility.",
      "Label it",
      "A tag on the valve helps the next person, including a guest. Write the off direction. In a second home, put the location with the electrical panel note.",
      "Residential plumbing includes replacing failed valves. If a valve weeps when you touch it, say so. Do not force a painted, seized valve when the leak can wait for a weekday visit.",
      ["Search the entry point and the meter.","Learn quarter-turn versus multi-turn.","Label the valve.","Call the water utility if a flood will not stop."])
    A("pinhole-leak","A pinhole in a supply line","Pipes","repiping",
      "How to limit damage from a pinhole, and how that differs from a re-pipe conversation.",
      "Small hole, steady pressure",
      "Supply lines stay under pressure. A pinhole can mist a cabinet for weeks. When it opens up, the flow is steady and not tied to a drain cycle. Shut the main if the spray is active.",
      "One pinhole on sound copper can be a local repair. A series of pinholes, or rusty galvanized along a whole run, is a re-pipe conversation. Pipe inspection is the listed way to look before opening a long wall.",
      "The heater",
      "Move stored items out of the wet area. Turn the water heater off only if the leak may have emptied it or water reached the controls. Firing an empty tank ruins it. Photos of the pipe and the room can go to office@plumbing101.net.",
      "Say the metal if you can tell copper, plastic, or crusty steel.",
      ["Shut the main on an active spray.","Note the pipe material.","Keep the heater full if the tank is not the leak.","Photo the damage."])
    A("hose-bibs","Hose bibs in salt air","Coastal homes","faucet-repair",
      "Why outdoor faucets fail on the Oregon Coast, and what a vacuum breaker does.",
      "Salt, hoses, and freezes",
      "A bib facing the ocean lives in salt spray. Handles seize and the joint at the wall can weep into the siding. A hose left on in a cold snap can hold water in the faucet body so it freezes, and it can defeat a frost-proof faucet. Take hoses off before a freeze and before a long vacancy.",
      "The small cap on many bibs is a vacuum breaker. It keeps hose water from siphoning back into the house. If it cracks or sticks, the faucet may still run, but the backflow protection is done.",
      "Stains under a window",
      "A stain that grows only when the bib is used is the bib leaking backward into the wall. A stain that grows only in wind-driven rain may be weather, not plumbing. Stop using a bib that wets the wall and call. Faucet repair is listed work.",
      "Say which side of the house takes the weather. Bluff towns such as Oceanside and Cape Meares age the ocean side first.",
      ["Remove hoses before a freeze or a long trip.","Note a cracked vacuum breaker.","Watch whether a wall stain grows when the bib is used.","Name the weather side of the house."])
    A("outdoor-showers","Outdoor showers at beach houses","Coastal homes","bathroom-plumbing",
      "Sand, shutoffs, and scald risk on outdoor showers.",
      "Sand is the clog",
      "Outdoor showers dump sand into a trap. A screen you can lift saves the line. A shower that stands in a puddle has a slow drain. A head that drips for an hour has a valve that is not closing. Those are a drain call and a faucet call.",
      "Mix valves outdoors need the same scald sense as indoor showers. A valve in full sun is a risk if the cold side fails. Pressure-balance valves belong on showers guests use.",
      "Winter",
      "An exterior shower should have a shutoff inside the heated space and a way to drain the exposed pipe. If you cannot find that shutoff, ask for one. A January night in Hebo is a harder freeze than a Lincoln City night at sea level, and ocean wind can still freeze an exposed line.",
      "Say the shower is outdoors so the estimate includes access and freeze protection. Bathroom plumbing and faucet repair are the listed services.",
      ["Clear sand from the screen.","Find the indoor shutoff.","Stop using a shower that will not close.","Say the shower is outdoors when you call."])
    A("crawlspace-water","Water under the house","Coastal homes","pipe-inspection",
      "How to tell rain water in a crawlspace from a pipe leak.",
      "Storm water is common here",
      "Lincoln and Tillamook counties take heavy rain. Crawlspaces wet down when grade slopes toward the house or gutters dump at the foundation. That water is not always a broken pipe. A supply leak is usually under a line, active while the water is on, and clearer than muddy groundwater.",
      "If it is safe, shut the house main and watch the wet spot. Groundwater does not stop because the main is off. A drain leak shows when a fixture is used and stays quiet when it is not.",
      "When not to crawl",
      "Do not enter a tight space if the structure worries you or if sewage is present. Black water and a sewer smell are a drain problem. After river flooding around Otis or Nehalem, leave heaters and electrical gear off and assume the water is contaminated.",
      "Pipe inspection is a listed service. Photos of the wet bay, the nearest pipe, and the outside grade help.",
      ["Shut the main and see if the drip stops.","Use one fixture and watch a suspected drain.","Stay out of sewage.","Photo the outside grade."])
    A("open-the-house","Opening a beach house","Coastal homes","residential-plumbing",
      "A walk-through before anyone uses a house that has been closed.",
      "Pressure, then listen",
      "Open the main slowly. Walk the lowest fixtures and the water heater. With every faucet off, listen. A toilet that refills has a leaking flapper or a stuck fill valve. A hiss in a wall is a reason to shut the main again and call.",
      "Fill traps that may have dried: showers, floor drains, and the laundry standpipe. Run a little hot water and look at the heater fittings. Supply lines that sat all winter sometimes fail on the first pressurization. Look under each sink.",
      "Before guests",
      "A running toilet can run for days in a rental before anyone reports it. A short walk now is the cheaper option. If something failed and the leak is stopped, email office@plumbing101.net with photos. For a live leak, call (541) 614-1344 on a weekday between 8:30 a.m. and 3:30 p.m.",
      "Residential plumbing, toilets, faucets, drains, and water heaters are all on the published list.",
      ["Open the main slowly.","Listen with fixtures off.","Refill dry traps.","Check under sinks and at the heater."])
    A("close-the-house","Leaving a house empty","Coastal homes","residential-plumbing",
      "Steps that limit leak damage while a coast house sits empty.",
      "Choose what stays on",
      "Shutting the main off stops a supply leak from running for weeks. If you shut the water off, shut the heater off too, or use the vacation setting only if the manufacturer allows it, so the unit does not fire dry. If you leave the water on for a neighbor, a failed line will run until someone notices.",
      "Take hoses off bibs. Fill traps before you leave, and accept that a very long vacancy may dry them again. Ask someone local to walk through if you will be gone a month or more.",
      "Skip these",
      "Do not fire a heater on an empty tank. Do not leave a washer full of standing water. Do not leave a chemical opener sitting in a trap. Write down where the main is so your person can use it.",
      "If you come back to a stain, a smell, or no hot water, use the phone line during posted hours.",
      ["Decide whether the main stays on.","Shut the heater if the water is off.","Remove hoses.","Leave shutoff directions."])
    A("after-a-storm","After a coastal windstorm","Coastal homes","pipe-inspection",
      "What to look over on the plumbing after hard wind and rain, from the ground.",
      "Vents and flashing",
      "From the ground, look at hose bibs, pipe under decks, and any roof vent you can see without climbing. A vent packed with debris can slow drains and suck traps dry. Do not climb a wet roof. Missing shingles over a vent are a roof problem that can wet the ceiling around the pipe.",
      "Inside, check ceilings around stacks and floors around toilets. If water appears only during rain and never when fixtures run, say that plainly. Pipe inspection covers the plumbing side. A roof leak needs a roofer.",
      "Power and gas",
      "Electric heaters come back when power returns. Gas heaters with electronic controls may need a reset from the printed instructions if the area is dry and you smell no gas. If roof water wet the heater, leave it off. For a gas smell, call the gas utility, not an estimate form.",
      "Call on the next weekday if a supply line, a drain, or a heater was damaged.",
      ["View vents from the ground.","Separate rain-only water from fixture water.","Leave a wet heater off.","Use the gas utility for a gas smell."])
    A("inland-freeze","Frozen pipes inland of the beach","Coastal homes","residential-plumbing",
      "Why valley towns freeze pipes that the shoreline often misses.",
      "Colder nights",
      "A Lincoln City cottage may stay above freezing on a night that splits a pipe in Hebo, Eddyville, or Siletz. Unheated crawlspaces, well houses, and exterior walls are the risk. Opening a vanity door on an outside wall lets room heat reach the pipes. That is a fair night-of step.",
      "A frost-proof faucet only drains if the hose is off and the valve seat is inside the warm wall. A faucet left dripping can protect a line you can watch. It can also hide a valve that no longer shuts.",
      "The thaw",
      "Open a faucet as the ice lets go and watch for stains. Splits often show up on the thaw, not at the coldest hour. Shut the main if water appears where it should not. Then call.",
      "Name the town, especially an inland valley, so the visit includes the crawlspace or well house.",
      ["Open vanity doors on outside walls.","Remove hoses from frost-proof faucets.","Shut the main if a thaw brings a leak.","Name the town."])
